          "ruleId": "CWE-346",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "undici's fetch() CORS enforcement is a no-op: corsCheck(request, response) in lib/web/fetch/util.js ignores both arguments and unconditionally `return 'success'` (body is just `// TODO` then the return). It is called at lib/web/fetch/index.js:1160 as the sole gate `if (request.responseTainting === 'cors' && corsCheck(request, response) === 'failure') return Promise.resolve(makeNetworkError('cors failure'))`. Since corsCheck can never return 'failure', a cross-origin response that does NOT grant Access-Control-Allow-Origin is never blocked by this path -- any caller relying on undici's fetch (e.g. Node's global fetch, which is undici-backed) to enforce CORS semantics for `mode: 'cors'` requests gets no actual enforcement here.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Source inspection confirms the claim exactly. `corsCheck` in lib/web/fetch/util.js (lines 226-229) is declared with no parameters, its body is only `// TODO` followed by `return 'success'`, so it ignores whatever `request`/`response` it's called with and can never signal failure. The sole call site, lib/web/fetch/index.js:1158-1163, uses it as the only gate before returning a network error: `if (request.responseTainting === 'cors' && corsCheck(request, response) === 'failure') { return makeNetworkError('cors failure') }` -- since corsCheck always returns 'success', this branch is dead code and no cross-origin response is ever rejected for missing Access-Control-Allow-Origin via this path. I also confirmed via request.js (fallbackMode = 'cors' at line 105, applied at line 268/281) that plain `fetch(url)` calls default to mode 'cors', so this is the default/common path, not an edge case. Graph query (run_cypher, scan_id scoped) confirms the call at index.js:1159-1162 is reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1, matching the analyst's reachability claim.\n\nHowever, this is NOT a secretly-introduced bug: the repo's own WPT conformance manifest (test/wpt/status/fetch.status.json) explicitly documents and skips the entire \"cors\" WPT test category and several mode-no-cors tests with the note \\\"undici doesn't implement CORs\\\" (lines ~47, ~126, ~432). This is a long-standing, intentionally unimplemented spec section acknowledged by the maintainers, not an accidental regression -- there is no CVE for it and exploit_search's corpus returned no matching module (catalog not indexed, so this is informational only, not a ruling). Practically, CORS is a browser same-origin/ambient-credential sandboxing mechanism; outside a browser's script-isolation model (plain Node.js server code calling its own fetch), there is normally no distinct \"attacker script\" actor for this gate to protect against, which is presumably why the undici team left it unimplemented rather than treating it as a vulnerability. I'm confirming the lead as an accurate description of real, reachable code behavior, but downgrading severity to LOW because it is a known/documented upstream design gap with limited realistic security impact in undici's primary (non-browser) use case, rather than a novel exploitable flaw."
          },
