        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-346",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy (CORP) enforcement is likewise stubbed: crossOriginResourcePolicyCheck() in lib/web/fetch/util.js takes no parameters, contains only `// TODO`, and unconditionally `return 'allowed'`. It is invoked at lib/web/fetch/index.js:1177-1185 with four real arguments (request.origin, request.client, request.destination, actualResponse) as the gate for opaque/cross-origin responses: `if ((request.responseTainting === 'opaque' || response.type === 'opaque') && crossOriginResourcePolicyCheck(...) === 'blocked') return Promise.resolve(makeNetworkError('blocked'))`. Because the function ignores its inputs and always returns 'allowed', a server's `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin`/`same-site` response header is never honored -- opaque cross-origin resource loads are never blocked.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Source inspection confirms the lead exactly. In lib/web/fetch/util.js:219-223, the function is defined with zero parameters and a body that is only a comment and an unconditional return: `function crossOriginResourcePolicyCheck () { // TODO\\n return 'allowed' }`. This is exported (util.js:1510) and imported into index.js (index.js:29). In lib/web/fetch/index.js, inside the async `httpFetch` handler (entry point, kind='handler', exposure='reachable' per graph), lines 1172-1186 implement WHATWG fetch spec step 7: `if ((request.responseTainting === 'opaque' || response.type === 'opaque') && crossOriginResourcePolicyCheck(request.origin, request.client, request.destination, actualResponse) === 'blocked') { return makeNetworkError('blocked') }`. Because the function ignores all four arguments and always returns the literal string 'allowed', the `=== 'blocked'` branch can never be taken, so a server's Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy header is never enforced for opaque cross-origin fetch responses \u2014 the gate is permanently open. Graph query confirms reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1 for the call node (uid f884287cac44498da8e20fa874eae2b33b675216) at index.js:1178, and this is undici itself (package.json version 6.11.0, this is the library's own source, not a third-party dependency) so the standard CVE/version patch-check does not apply here; the exploit corpus was also unavailable (not indexed) so no exploit-reference cross-check could be run. Note the sibling functions corsCheck() and TAOCheck() in the same file (util.js:226-234) are stubbed identically, confirming this is a systemic pattern of unimplemented WHATWG security checks in this fetch implementation, not an isolated typo."
          },
