        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-200",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Response header filtering for cross-origin fetch responses does not implement the spec's safelist: filterResponse() in lib/web/fetch/response.js, for both 'basic' and 'cors' filtered response types, passes through the entire header list unchanged with explicit comments 'Note: undici does not implement forbidden response-header names' and 'Note: undici does not implement CORS-safelisted response-header names'. Combined with the always-passing corsCheck above, this means script code reading a cross-origin Response's headers via a CORS-mode fetch gets every response header (not just the CORS-safelisted set plus anything listed in Access-Control-Expose-Headers), an information-exposure gap relative to the Fetch spec's intended header confidentiality boundary.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Source inspection confirms the claim exactly. In lib/web/fetch/response.js, filterResponse(response, type) (called from the main fetch algorithm in index.js:663-668, keyed off request.responseTainting \u2014 i.e. 'basic' for same-origin, 'cors' for cross-origin CORS-mode fetches) has two branches that the analyst described verbatim: for type==='basic' it returns makeFilteredResponse(response, {type:'basic', headersList: response.headersList}) preceded by the comment \"Note: undici does not implement forbidden response-header names\"; for type==='cors' it does the identical pass-through of the full, unfiltered headersList preceded by \"Note: undici does not implement CORS-safelisted response-header names\". Neither branch removes forbidden response-header names (e.g. Set-Cookie/Set-Cookie2) nor restricts to the CORS-safelisted set + Access-Control-Expose-Headers as fetch spec \u00a7concept-filtered-response requires. I further verified downstream that nothing else in the Headers class compensates: Headers.get/has/getSetCookie()/iteration in lib/web/fetch/headers.js never consult kGuard to strip forbidden response headers (the guard checks are themselves stubbed with \"Note: undici does not implement forbidden header names\"), so a cross-origin CORS-mode fetch's Set-Cookie would remain readable via response.headers.getSetCookie(). I also independently verified the analyst's secondary claim: corsCheck() in lib/web/fetch/util.js:225-229 is a literal stub \u2014 `function corsCheck () { // TODO; return 'success' }` \u2014 it always succeeds regardless of Access-Control-Allow-Origin, so the only spec gate before filterResponse runs is a no-op. Graph query confirms reachability: the filterResponse CpgCall node (lib/web/fetch/response.js, name=filterResponse) has reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1, i.e. it sits on the live path from an entry point, consistent with it being invoked on every httpFetch call. This is a genuine, currently-reachable spec-compliance gap, not dead code."
          },
