        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-20",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Headers.append/delete/set in lib/web/fetch/headers.js do not implement the Fetch spec's 'forbidden header name' check: appendHeader() contains the comment 'Note: undici does not implement forbidden header names' immediately after the spec steps that would normally silently drop forbidden header names when a Headers object's guard is 'request' (e.g. Host, Cookie, Origin, Via, Proxy-*, Sec-Fetch-*, Content-Length). Because this allowlist/blocklist step is skipped, any code path that lets external input set headers on a Request/fetch() call (via a Headers instance with guard 'request') can set normally-forbidden, security-relevant request headers.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from source (not from the analyst's say-so). lib/web/fetch/headers.js:83-118 (appendHeader), :368-411 (Headers.delete), and :454-504 (Headers.set) all contain the exact spec steps \"3. If headers's guard is 'immutable', then throw... 4. Otherwise, if headers's guard is 'request' and name is a forbidden header name, return.\" followed verbatim by \"// Note: undici does not implement forbidden header names\" \u2014 and in all three the forbidden-name branch is genuinely absent; only the 'immutable' throw and an empty 'request-no-cors' TODO remain. Confirmed in the graph too: CpgMethod lib/web/fetch/headers.js::program:appendHeader has line=83, reachable_from_entry=true. Confirmed guard='request' is really assigned to every Request's headers object at lib/web/fetch/request.js:438 (`this[kHeaders][kGuard] = 'request'`), so the skipped check is live on exactly the Headers instances the spec means to restrict. This is a real, reachable spec-compliance gap, not a misread comment.\n\nHowever the analyst's \"security-relevant\" framing needs correction/narrowing after tracing the actual network path: the specific example they lead with (Host) is NOT actually forgeable over the wire \u2014 lib/web/fetch/index.js:1540 unconditionally does `httpRequest.headersList.delete('host', true)` right before the HTTP-network-or-cache fetch step, and lib/dispatcher/client-h1.js:964-970 derives the literal `host:` line solely from `request.host` (the real connection target), never from the user-supplied headers array. So Host/Origin spoofing via this gap is mitigated elsewhere. Cookie and the Via/Proxy-*/Sec-Fetch-* headers ARE NOT stripped outside of the cross-origin-redirect step (index.js:1326-1333 only deletes cookie/host on redirect to a different origin), so they do pass through unfiltered on a direct fetch()/Request \u2014 a genuine, if narrower, header-injection surface. Content-Length is the most concretely exploitable leftover: index.js:1449 does `httpRequest.headersList.append('content-length', realLength, true)` without first checking/deleting any attacker-set 'content-length', and HeadersList.append() (headers.js:168-190) concatenates onto an existing value with ', ' rather than overwriting, so a forged Content-Length set via Headers.set() survives into client-h1.js's header-writing loop (client-h1.js:980-993, which writes every header pair verbatim with no forbidden-name filtering) as a single malformed/dual-valued Content-Length line \u2014 a genuine, if narrow, HTTP request-smuggling-adjacent primitive. Net effect: the code defect is real and exploitable for Cookie/Content-Length-class headers reaching a direct (non-redirected) request; the Host-spoofing portion of the claim is mitigated at dispatch. exploit_search corpus was unavailable/not indexed so no published-exploit corroboration was possible either way."
          },
