        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-200",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "The low-level dispatcher's redirect handler strips fewer sensitive headers on a cross-origin redirect than the newer Fetch-spec implementation does. lib/handler/redirect-handler.js:shouldRemoveHeader() only removes 'host' (always), 'content-*' (on 303), and 'authorization'/'cookie' (when origin changed) -- it does not strip 'proxy-authorization'. By contrast lib/web/fetch/index.js (its own, separate cross-origin-redirect header cleanup at lines 1326-1333) explicitly does `request.headersList.delete('proxy-authorization', true)` in addition to authorization/cookie/host. Any caller using the classic Client/Pool/Agent + RedirectHandler API (not fetch()) with a 'proxy-authorization' header set on the original request will have that header forwarded verbatim to a different-origin redirect target.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently read both files (undici v6.11.0, verified via package.json). lib/handler/redirect-handler.js:197-209 `shouldRemoveHeader()` only strips 'host' (len===4), 'content-*' (on 303), and 'authorization'/'cookie' (len 13/6, when origin changed) \u2014 there is no branch matching 'proxy-authorization' (len 20), so it falls through to `return false` and is kept. By contrast, lib/web/fetch/index.js's cross-origin-redirect step (lines 1321-1334, guarded by `!sameOrigin(...)`) explicitly calls `request.headersList.delete('proxy-authorization', true)` (line 1329) alongside authorization/cookie/host, per the Fetch spec's 'authentication-entries' clause (cited in the code comment). The docs (docs/docs/best-practices/proxy.md) show the documented, supported pattern of setting a literal 'proxy-authorization' header on `client.request()` opts. Redirect-following via the classic dispatcher API is opt-in through `maxRedirections` (default 0, confirmed in lib/dispatcher/client.js/agent.js and lib/interceptor/redirect(-interceptor).js), and whenever it's enabled, RedirectHandler.onHeaders() calls cleanRequestHeaders\u2192shouldRemoveHeader on every cross-origin redirect. Graph query confirms shouldRemoveHeader/cleanRequestHeaders are reachable_from_entry=true (hop_distance 0) from the repo's own usage entry points. exploit_search corpus was unavailable (not indexed) so no CVE/module cross-check could be performed, but source evidence alone is conclusive and matches the analyst's citation almost exactly."
          },
