        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-346",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Timing-Allow-Origin (TAO) enforcement is also a stub: TAOCheck() in lib/web/fetch/util.js takes no parameters and unconditionally `return 'success'`, called at lib/web/fetch/index.js:1167 (`if (TAOCheck(request, response) === 'failure') request.timingAllowFailed = true`). Since it can never return 'failure', `timingAllowFailed` is never set from this path, so cross-origin timing information is never withheld via this check regardless of the actual Timing-Allow-Origin response header. Lower severity than the CORS/CORP gaps (affects only Resource Timing API precision/leak), included for completeness of the same stubbed-check pattern.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from source: TAOCheck() in lib/web/fetch/util.js (lines 232-235) takes no parameters and unconditionally returns 'success' (body is just `// TODO` + `return 'success'`). It is called at lib/web/fetch/index.js:1167 as `if (TAOCheck(request, response) === 'failure') { request.timingAllowFailed = true }` \u2014 since the function ignores its arguments and can never return 'failure', this branch is dead code and `timingAllowFailed` is never set via this path. I traced the downstream effect to confirm real impact (not just a dead branch): index.js:685-689 sets `response.timingAllowPassed = true` whenever `request.timingAllowFailed` is falsy \u2014 which, given the stub, is always. That flag is consumed at index.js:281 and :1014 to decide whether resource timing info is coarsened/opaque (per the Resource-Timing TAO-check spec intent) or exposed in full detail via `markResourceTiming`/`performance.markResourceTiming`. So the stub causes full-fidelity cross-origin timing data to always be surfaced, independent of the actual response's Timing-Allow-Origin header \u2014 a genuine, functioning bypass of the TAO check, matching the analyst's claim exactly. Graph query also confirms the call site is reachable_from_entry=true (hop_distance=1), strengthening exploitability beyond what the analyst cited."
          },
