        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-346",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "In undici's fetch implementation, the three real cross-origin security checks used by httpFetch \u2014 corsCheck(), TAOCheck() (Timing-Allow-Origin check), and crossOriginResourcePolicyCheck() \u2014 are stub functions that unconditionally return a 'pass' value ('success'/'success'/'allowed') with only a `// TODO` comment in place of the actual spec logic. httpFetch (lib/web/fetch/index.js, reachable_from_entry=true) calls these at lines 1159-1178 to decide whether to reject a cross-origin response (`corsCheck(...) === 'failure'`, `TAOCheck(...) === 'failure'`, `crossOriginResourcePolicyCheck(...) === 'blocked'`), but since the callees always return the non-blocking value, these guards can never fire \u2014 cross-origin response filtering, Timing-Allow-Origin enforcement, and Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy blocking are effectively permanently disabled for any caller relying on this fetch() implementation's CORS semantics.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Verified directly against real source (not just the graph). lib/web/fetch/util.js:220-235 defines crossOriginResourcePolicyCheck, corsCheck, and TAOCheck as unconditional stubs \u2014 each body is only a `// TODO` comment followed by `return 'allowed'`/`'success'`/`'success'` respectively, with no parameters even consulted. lib/web/fetch/index.js's httpFetch (line 1123, confirmed reachable_from_entry=true via mcp__orion__run_cypher) calls them exactly as the analyst described: line 1160 `corsCheck(request, response) === 'failure'` gating a cors-failure network error, line 1167 `TAOCheck(request, response) === 'failure'` gating the timing-allow-failed flag, and lines 1178-1183 `crossOriginResourcePolicyCheck(...) === 'blocked'` gating the CORP network error. Since the callees are hardcoded to never return 'failure'/'blocked', none of these three guards can ever trigger \u2014 real cross-origin filtering, TAO timing-exposure gating, and CORP blocking are dead code paths. Graph query confirms all three util.js methods and httpFetch itself have reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0, and there is a public EntryPoint `index.js::program:fetch` (kind=handler, exposure=reachable) that leads into this code, so the finding is reachable from the package's public API, not merely theoretical. (Note: exploit_search corpus was unavailable/not indexed in this environment, so no Metasploit-module corroboration could be obtained; this does not affect the source-level confirmation.) Severity is rated MEDIUM rather than HIGH because exploitability is context-dependent \u2014 undici's fetch() is primarily used server-to-server in Node.js where there is no browser-style shared-origin script sandbox for CORS/CORP/TAO to protect, so impact is realized only when this fetch() implementation backs a context that relies on it for actual cross-origin isolation (e.g. embedding in sandboxed/multi-tenant script execution, Electron-style renderers, or test/polyfill environments emulating browser fetch semantics)."
          },
