            "text": "Declared dependency ws at range ^8.11.0 (allows resolving to any 8.x release from 8.11.0 up to <9.0.0) is vulnerable to CVE-2024-37890, a ReDoS in ws's parsing of the Sec-WebSocket-Extensions request header for versions prior to 8.17.1. Because the declared semver range permits installation of any pre-8.17.1 8.x version, a lockfile/install that hasn't been bumped since 8.11.0 would ship the vulnerable parser. This is a components-with-known-vulnerabilities finding independent of any in-repo request flow.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Verified directly against source and graph: package.json declares \"ws\": \"^8.11.0\" under devDependencies (confirmed at line 121), and the graph's Dependency node matches exactly (MATCH (d:Dependency {scan_id,name:'ws'}) -> {\"name\":\"ws\",\"version\":\"^8.11.0\"}). This semver range (>=8.11.0 <9.0.0) permits resolution to any pre-8.17.1 8.x release, which is publicly documented as vulnerable to a ReDoS in ws's Sec-WebSocket-Extensions header parser (CVE-2024-37890 / GHSA-3h5v-q93c-6h6q, fixed in 8.17.1) \u2014 this is well-established public CVE knowledge consistent with the analyst's description; the exploit_search corpus was not indexed in this environment so it could not be used to double-check the version range, but the claim does not rely on a weaponized Metasploit module. No lockfile (package-lock.json/yarn.lock/pnpm-lock.yaml) exists in this repo, so the exact resolved version cannot be pinned \u2014 the range genuinely permits (but does not guarantee) a vulnerable install. Important correction to the analyst's implied risk: this repo is the `undici` project itself (package.json name=\"undici\"), and `ws` is declared ONLY as a devDependency, used exclusively in test files (test/websocket/*.js, test/fixtures/websocket.js) to spin up a local WebSocketServer for exercising undici's own WebSocket client, plus one doc example. devDependencies are never installed for consumers of the published `undici` package, so this has zero exposure to anyone depending on undici in production \u2014 the only exposure is to a contributor's/CI's local test run, and even then only if an attacker-controlled client could reach that local test server's Sec-WebSocket-Extensions parsing, which is not an externally reachable path. This downgrades real-world severity substantially versus a production dependency finding."
